   "source": [
    "# 00 · Getting the data\n",
    "\n",
    "Run this notebook top to bottom once. It gets the data onto your machine and shows how to use it.\n",
    "After that, any notebook can load the data with three lines (section 4)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-01",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Our goal\n",
    "\n",
    "**Does the text of a company's annual report predict how volatile its share price will be over\n",
    "the next year, beyond what last year's volatility already tells us?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Last year's volatility is already a strong predictor, so that is our baseline. We build neural\n",
    "networks that read the report and see whether they beat it. The full plan is in\n",
    "[`Oscar/PLAN.md`](../Oscar/PLAN.md)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-02",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. What the data is\n",
    "\n",
    "**27,142 annual reports** from US public companies, filed 1996–2006. For each report we have:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Two numbers about the share price:**\n",
    "  - `logvol_before`: volatility over the 12 months before the report. **The baseline input.**\n",
    "  - `logvol_after`: volatility over the 12 months after. **What we predict.**\n",
    "\n",
    "  Both are logs, which is why they're negative (around −3.4 typically).\n",
    "- **Two sections of the report's text, already turned into numbers:**\n",
    "  - **Item 1**, the business description: what the company does, its markets and competitors.\n",
    "  - **Item 7**, management's discussion of the year.\n",
    "\n",
    "  Each section is cut into **chunks** of about 400 words, and each chunk has been turned into a\n",
    "  list of **768 numbers** that captures what it says. A typical section is 14–16 chunks, at most 64.\n",
    "  So each report's text is a short sequence of 768-number vectors per section."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. How to get it\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything is in **one file, `data.pkl`** (about 1.6 GB).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Step 1: add the shared OneDrive folder.** Oscar has shared a OneDrive folder called **`sprint2-data`**\n",
    "with you (check your Bristol email or OneDrive's *Shared* page). Open it in OneDrive on the web and\n",
    "click **Add shortcut to My files**. It then syncs to your computer like your other OneDrive files.\n",
    "Right-click `data.pkl` and choose **Always keep on this device** so the copy below doesn't stall.\n",
    "Don't edit the files in it: it's everyone's copy.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Step 2: set up Python**, if you haven't already. From the repo root:\n",
    "`python3 -m venv .venv`, `source .venv/bin/activate`, `pip install -r requirements.txt`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Step 3: run the cell below.** It finds the folder and copies `data.pkl` into `data/processed/` in\n",
    "your copy of the repo, which is where our code looks. If it can't find the folder, paste the folder's\n",
    "path into `DATA_DIR` (on a Mac: right-click the folder in Finder, hold Option, *Copy as Pathname*).\n",
    "Never commit the file: `data/processed/` is in `.gitignore` already."
   ]
  },
